= Spelt

_Ayurvedic status: not classical — reasoned analysis only, on the broader godhūma (wheat) axis. Chinese: not classical — reasoned analysis only, on the 小麥 (xiǎomài) wheat axis. Greco-Roman / European: a full classical pharmacy under far / zea / olyra / spelta, with a notable medieval expansion in Hildegard von Bingen._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Triticum spelta_ L., family Poaceae — a hexaploid hulled wheat (the grain remains in its persistent glumes after threshing and must be dehulled). Distinct from the naked hexaploid common/bread wheat _T. aestivum_ and from the tetraploid hulled emmer _T. dicoccum_ (= _T. turgidum_ subsp. _dicoccon_), with which it is often conflated in ancient and modern sources. The classical Roman cereal _far_ (gen. _farris_) covered hulled wheats — primarily emmer, the staple of the early Republic, and probably also spelt where the two co-occurred; the texts seldom distinguish. The late-Latin word _spelta_ first appears clearly in the late 3rd–4th century CE (notably in Diocletian's _Edictum de pretiis_, 301 CE) and is of Germanic origin (cognate with Old High German _spelza_ / _spelta_). _Triticum_ itself derives from _terere_ ("to rub, thresh"); _frumentum_ is the general grain word; _farreus_ "of _far_" gives the Roman marriage rite _confarreatio_ (the sharing of a _far_-cake).#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _spelta_, _far_, _triticum_, _frumentum_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _far_, _tero_); _Oxford Latin Dictionary_. First clear attestation of _spelta_ in Diocletian's _Edictum de pretiis_ (301 CE). Species identity (hexaploid _T. spelta_ vs. tetraploid emmer _T. dicoccum_) is botanically straightforward; the conflation in the ancient sources is well documented in Roman agronomy scholarship — see Spurr, _Arable Cultivation in Roman Italy c. 200 B.C. to c. A.D. 100_ (1986).]

*English:* _Spelt_, with one Old English attestation (a Vespasian Psalter gloss, c. 8th–9th c.), then largely absent from English until the late 16th c., when the word was re-borrowed from Late Latin / German. Cognate with Old High German _spelza_/_spelta_, Modern German _Spelt_/_Dinkel_, Dutch _spelt_. The further IE etymology is uncertain; the folk derivation from "to split" (i.e. the splitting of the glumes in dehulling) is intuitively attractive but unproven and not accepted by the OED.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "spelt, n.¹"); Kluge, _Etymologisches Wörterbuch der deutschen Sprache_ (s.v. _Spelt_, _Dinkel_). The "split" folk etymology is flagged as folk — true IE derivation uncertain.]

*Chinese:* No classical entry. Modern descriptive terms: 斯佩耳特小麥 (sīpèi'ěrtè xiǎomài, phonetic + "wheat"), 古麥 (gǔmài, "ancient wheat") informally, 二粒小麥 (èrlì xiǎomài, "two-grain wheat") more strictly for emmer but loosely for hulled wheats. The classical canon's 小麥 (xiǎomài) is unambiguously _T. aestivum_; spelt has no traditional Chinese drug name.#footnote[Modern Chinese terms: standard botanical/agronomic vocabulary; cf. _Flora of China_ (Poaceae). The non-existence of a classical Chinese entry for spelt is noted explicitly; statements below under the Chinese-medicine heading are reasoned analysis on the 小麥 axis, not citation.]

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry. The Sanskrit canon's wheat term गोधूम (_godhūma_) does not distinguish hulled from naked wheats, and spelt was not cultivated in South Asia in the classical period. Modern Hindi/Sanskrit usage: स्पेल्ट (_spelṭ_, transliteration), or descriptively जर्मन गेहूँ (_jarman gehūm̐_, "German wheat"). Honest: no classical Sanskrit identification.#footnote[The absence of spelt from the South Asian classical canon is botanical/agricultural fact — spelt is a Eurasian temperate-cool crop that did not enter Indian cultivation classically. Sanskrit _godhūma_ subsumes wheat generically; specific cultivar distinctions are post-classical.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Reasoned placement on the 小麥 axis — not a classical Chinese entry. Spelt is a closely related hexaploid hulled wheat; classical Chinese dietetics has no separate drug for it. The placement below extends the 小麥 / 浮小麥 doctrine to a related grain by inference, and should be read as comparative rather than canonical._

By analogy with the canonical 小麥 entry: spelt would be expected to share the same broad axis — sweet (甘) flavor; cool (涼) to neutral property in the whole grain (slightly cooler than fully refined modern wheat, because of intact bran and germ); Heart (心), Spleen (脾), Kidney (腎) channels.#footnote[Wheat axis as cited: _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 小麥 entry; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004). The application to spelt is interpretive and is flagged as such.]

*Expected actions and indications (by analogy):* 養心安神 (nourishes Heart and calms _shén_), 除煩 (eliminates vexation), 益腎、養液 (benefits Kidney, nourishes fluids), 厚腸胃 (strengthens Stomach and intestines, when properly cooked). The hulled-grain status — bran and germ kept through threshing — is expected to *temper* the cloying / damp-generating tendency that refined wheat flour acquires in dietetics; spelt is plausibly read as a less damp-generating wheat. This is interpretive.

*Cautions:* sweet and moistening; as a wheat, expected to be unsuitable in heavy quantities for damp-excess or for Spleen-vacuity-damp constitutions. Gluten content (as the modern correlate of the _zàngzào_-soothing tradition is the wheat-protein matrix) is essentially comparable to that of common wheat — so the same caution for celiac / non-celiac gluten sensitivity applies (this is modern, not classical).#footnote[The damp-generating / refined-flour caution is the standard TCM dietetic point (cf. Kastner, _Chinese Nutrition Therapy_); the gluten / celiac point is modern (M) and flagged explicitly.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis on the godhūma axis. Spelt has no classical Sanskrit name; what follows extends the godhūma dravyaguna to a closely related hulled wheat. Flagged as interpretive throughout._

By analogy with _godhūma_ (Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 — _annapānavidhi_; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_):

*Rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Guṇa:* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous) — slightly modified by the intact bran of dehulled-but-whole spelt, which contributes a small rūkṣa-laghu (drier, lighter) note relative to fully refined wheat flour.

*Vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — godhūma is cooling in Ayurveda (contrast: warming in the Greco-Roman humoral tradition; the divergence is real and should not be smoothed over).

*Vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of godhūma: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, _dhānya-varga_, wheat verses); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba). The intact-bran modification for spelt is interpretive — flagged. The cooling–warming divergence between Ayurveda and Greco-Roman humoral theory for wheat is well known: cf. Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_, on _pyrós_ as warming and moistening, against Caraka's cool / heavy / unctuous.]

*Prabhāva:* No distinct prabhāva — neither classical (because the substance is not in the canon) nor reasoned (because its actions follow predictably from sweet-cooling-heavy-unctuous on the wheat axis). Flagged explicitly as "no prabhāva" rather than padded.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Pitta (vāta-pitta-śāmaka); increases Kapha in excess or in pre-existing Kapha conditions — as with godhūma.

*Karma (by analogy with godhūma):* _Brṃhaṇa_ (anabolic/nourishing), _balya_ (strengthening), _hṛdya_ (cardiotonic), _vṛṣya_ (mildly aphrodisiac/_śukrala_), _snehana_ (mildly unctuous-nourishing); supportive in convalescence and in Vāta-/Pitta-depletion states. Hulled-whole-grain spelt would be expected — interpretively — to be slightly less dampening than refined wheat flour.#footnote[Karma of godhūma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27, and Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_. The spelt-specific modifications are reasoned and are flagged as such.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(—) No named classical viruddha for spelt specifically* — none can exist, as the substance is not named in the canon. Wheat-axis (_godhūma_) cautions apply by inference: combination with heating-pungent and certain incompatible preparations as classically listed for wheat.
- *(S/N for godhūma):* incompatibility with certain heavy/heating pairings (e.g. some texts caution wheat with palmyra-sap and with certain fish/meat combinations); these are inherited by extension to spelt, not directly attested.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Celiac disease (autoimmune):* spelt contains gluten — gliadin epitopes _are_ present, including immunoreactive sequences; spelt is *not* safe for celiac disease. The widespread popular claim that spelt is celiac-tolerated is *false*. Some celiacs tolerate spelt subjectively but immunologically the disease is provoked.
  - *Non-celiac gluten/wheat sensitivity:* mixed reports; some patients tolerate spelt better than modern bread wheat, plausibly because spelt's *FODMAP* (fermentable oligo-/di-/monosaccharide and polyol) content — chiefly fructans — is lower than that of common wheat, depending on cultivar and processing; this is *not* a gluten effect and does not extend to celiac disease.
  - *Wheat allergy (IgE):* spelt cross-reacts with wheat.#footnote[Gluten content of spelt: confirmed by immunological assays — spelt gliadins contain the celiac-immunoreactive 33-mer α-gliadin peptide and related epitopes; reviewed in gluten-disorder literature (verify against current systematic reviews in _Gastroenterology_, _American Journal of Gastroenterology_, and _Nutrients_). The FODMAP/fructan point: see Ziegler et al. and related cereal-FODMAP analyses — fructan content is cultivar- and process-dependent; "spelt is gluten-free" is a popular misconception, flagged as such (M). The popular claim's distinction from the genuine FODMAP signal is important.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Spelt is *not classical in Tibb* as a distinct named drug. The substance is a Eurasian temperate-zone hulled wheat, geographically peripheral to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world; the Tibb literature does not distinguish it from the generic *qamḥ* (wheat) class. Modern Unani usage treats spelt by extension on the wheat axis (see wheat.typ). Tibb classification by extension: Hot and Moist in the 1st degree, paralleling generic qamḥ; the *jīr* (emmer) and *ʿalas* (German einkorn) variant grain-names in some medieval Arabic agricultural literature may include spelt within their semantic range, but the identification is not consistent. For the substantive Tibb treatment of the wheat axis, see _wheat.typ_. For the *Galenic / European* classical tradition of hulled wheats (the Greco-Roman _zeiá / ólyra / far / alica_ and Hildegard's _Dinkel_), see the Western / Greco-Roman section above — the Greco-Arabic and Hildegardian traditions are the substantive medieval-European pharmacology for spelt.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use spelt — see above. The closest tradition-internal analogue is 甘麥大棗湯 (_Gān Mài Dà Zǎo Tāng_), the wheat-based "Licorice, Wheat, & Jujube Decoction" of the _Jīnguì Yàolüè_, for _zàngzào_ (restless-organ disorder); spelt cannot be substituted on classical authority, and any such use is a modern dietary adaptation, not a tradition-grounded formula.#footnote[甘麥大棗湯 from Zhang Zhongjing, _Jīnguì Yàolüè_ (金匱要略); cf. Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009). The "spelt as wheat substitute" framing is modern dietary, not classical.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas use spelt. By inference on the godhūma axis, spelt would be appropriate in wheat-based _odana_ (rice/grain preparations), _pāyasa_, and _kṣīrapāka_-style milk-grain decoctions, and as a flour in _maṇḍa_ / _peyā_ / _vilepī_ convalescent preparations classically described for wheat. These are interpretive, not citation.#footnote[Wheat preparations in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_) and Sharangadhara Saṃhitā (preparation chapters); the application to spelt is interpretive.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

This is where spelt has a continuous documentary tradition.

- *Greco-Roman (with the emmer/spelt conflation caveat):* The Greek terms _ζειά_ (_zeiá_) and _ὄλυρα_ (_ólyra_) cover hulled wheats — emmer and probably spelt; _Theophrastus_ (_Hist. Plant._ VIII) discusses _zeiá_; _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_ II.85, _zeiá_; II.86, _ólyra_) treats them as nutritive and gently demulcent, the gruels suitable for convalescents. _Galen_ (_On the Properties of Foodstuffs_ I) discusses _zeiá_ and _ólyra_ as foods of middle nutritional grade — less fine than naked bread wheat (_pyrós_), more nourishing than barley (_krīthḗ_); he places wheat in general as warm and moist in his humoral system (the divergence with Ayurveda's cooling reading is real and should be noted, not smoothed over). _Pliny_ (_Naturalis Historia_ XVIII) is the long Latin source for _far_, _alica_, and _puls_.
- *Roman cereal practice:* _Far_ (emmer, and likely spelt where it grew) was the Republican staple, eaten chiefly as _puls_ (porridge); the Romans developed _alica_ (an emmer/spelt groats product, processed by parching, dehulling, and grinding — Pliny describes the manufacture in detail). _Confarreatio_, the most solemn Roman marriage rite, took its name from the _far_-cake (_libum farreum_) shared by bride and groom — embedding hulled wheat into Roman religion at the foundational level.
- *Medieval / Hildegard:* The clearest medieval pharmacy of spelt specifically (Latin _spelta_, German _Dinkel_) is in _Hildegard von Bingen_ (_Physica_, late 12th c.). Hildegard praises _Dinkel_ as the best grain for human flesh — warming, fattening in a wholesome sense, easy on the stomach, restoring strength to the sick; she prescribes spelt gruel for the ill and convalescent. The praise is medical-monastic dietetics, not formula-pharmacy, but it became the textual anchor of all modern Western "spelt revival" claims.
- *Galenic / pharmacy survivals:* spelt does not have a named officinal preparation in the modern pharmacopoeial sense; it persists as a food and as the basis of artisanal flour and pasta. In recent decades it has been revived in European and North-American organic agriculture and dietetics as an "ancient grain."#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Theophrastus, _Historia Plantarum_ VIII; Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ II.85–86 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_, Book I (Powell translation, Cambridge); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XVIII (Loeb / Rackham). _Alica_ manufacture: Pliny, _N.H._ XVIII.29. _Confarreatio_: standard Roman religion handbooks (e.g. Beard, North & Price, _Religions of Rome_). Hildegard: _Physica_, Book I, on _Dinkel_ (German edition: Schipperges, _Hildegard von Bingen Heilkunde_, and the English Throop translation, _Physica_). Modern revival as a popular/dietetic phenomenon — well documented but not a "tradition" in the classical sense.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, dehulled whole spelt grain, approximate values per 100 g dry grain — *verify against USDA FoodData Central* (current edition); these vary by cultivar and milling.#footnote[Composition figures are approximate and should be checked against USDA FoodData Central (https://fdc.nal.usda.gov), current edition; published values for _T. spelta_ vary by cultivar, season, and milling.]

- Energy ~340 kcal; Water ~11 g
- Protein ~14–15 g (somewhat higher than common bread wheat by total mass; gluten present)
- Fat ~2.4 g (notably a touch higher than common wheat; whole grain only)
- Carbohydrate ~70 g (mostly starch)
- Dietary fiber ~10 g (whole grain)
- Significant magnesium, manganese, zinc, niacin, thiamine, copper
- Phytic acid (as with all whole grains; reduced by sourdough/long fermentation)

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Gluten:* present; immunoreactive to celiac disease (above). The "spelt is gluten-free" claim is *false*. Spelt's protein matrix has a *somewhat different* gliadin profile (the ratio of α-/γ-/ω-gliadins differs from modern bread wheat), and the dough properties differ — but the celiac-relevant epitopes are present.
- *FODMAP / fructan content:* somewhat lower than modern bread wheat in many cultivars, especially after long sourdough fermentation; this is the *plausible* explanation for the popular subjective tolerability claim in non-celiac contexts. Cultivar- and process-dependent.
- *Phytic acid and minerals:* whole spelt has phytic acid (as does any whole-grain wheat) — this binds minerals; sourdough fermentation hydrolyses phytate and increases mineral bioavailability. A classical-modern convergence: traditional long fermentation (Roman _siligineus_ leavened breads, European sourdough) prepares the grain in a way modern phytochemistry rationalises.
- *Lectins (WGA):* present, as in all wheats; cooked/baked grain has the standard wheat-lectin profile. Popular claims of dramatic lectin-driven harms in normal preparations are not supported.#footnote[Gluten / gliadin epitope profile of spelt: published immunological studies (verify against current literature in _Gastroenterology_ and _Nutrients_). FODMAP fructan analyses for spelt vs. modern wheat: Ziegler and colleagues and related cereal-FODMAP studies — flag cultivar and process dependence. Phytate / mineral / sourdough literature: standard cereal science. WGA: standard wheat phytochemistry — the popular "lectin theory" of dietary harm is not supported by mainstream nutrition science and is flagged.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* No toxicological upper limit — spelt is a whole food. Practical considerations rather than a toxic ceiling: as a wheat, spelt is *contraindicated* in celiac disease (absolute, immunological) and (relatively) in established non-celiac gluten/wheat sensitivity, and contributes to the general dietary-pattern guidance on whole grains (~3 servings/day of whole grains is a common dietary target, not a safety threshold). For most adults: a routine serving of cooked spelt (~150–200 g cooked grain, or ~50 g flour) raises no specific safety question. Risk groups: celiac disease (absolute avoidance); IgE wheat allergy (avoidance); FODMAP-sensitive IBS (case-by-case).#footnote[Dietary-pattern guidance on whole grains varies by country and is dietary-pattern advice, not a safety limit. The celiac contraindication is absolute and well established; verify against current dietary and gastroenterology guidelines (e.g. American College of Gastroenterology celiac guidelines; EFSA/USDA dietary documents).]

